WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:04.440
The newest adaptation of Stephen King's It
contains a lot of Easter eggs — some of

00:00:04.440 --> 00:00:06.500
which are more obvious than others.

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:10.650
In addition to the ones we've talked about
before, like those t-shirt logos, here's a

00:00:10.650 --> 00:00:15.429
look at some of the other small details in
It that only true fans noticed.

00:00:15.429 --> 00:00:17.430
The absentee parents

00:00:17.430 --> 00:00:22.140
As we see in the beginning of the movie, Derry
has instituted a town curfew in reaction to

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:26.320
the disappearances of Georgie Denbrough, Betty
Ripsom, and others.

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:28.410
Kids are expected to be home by seven sharp.

00:00:28.410 --> 00:00:32.960
But that appears to be the full extent of
the adults' concern or responsive action regarding

00:00:32.960 --> 00:00:34.820
their own children's well-being.

00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:38.820
This is definitely a conscious nod to the
parents depicted in the book, who prefer to

00:00:38.820 --> 00:00:43.110
forget about the town's dark history, even
ignoring violence as it happens.

00:00:43.110 --> 00:00:47.150
Most of the parental figures in the film are
ones that have created additional problems

00:00:47.150 --> 00:00:48.790
for their children.

00:00:48.790 --> 00:00:49.880
Robert Dohay's head

00:00:49.880 --> 00:00:54.510
​During the movie's visual history of the
Easter parade explosion that claimed the lives

00:00:54.510 --> 00:00:59.160
of so many kids, there's one particularly
gruesome image that the camera seems to linger

00:00:59.160 --> 00:01:01.420
on: a boy's head up in a tree.

00:01:01.420 --> 00:01:02.420
"What's that?!"

00:01:02.420 --> 00:01:06.260
That's a nod to Robert Dohay, a nine-year-old
character from the book whose remains popped

00:01:06.260 --> 00:01:10.640
up days after the rest of the victims in a
neighbor's apple tree… and it was just his

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:11.640
head.

00:01:11.640 --> 00:01:14.750
Considering all the creepy book deaths that
didn't make the leap from the page to the

00:01:14.750 --> 00:01:18.130
big screen, that's an unsettling one to keep
for sure.

00:01:18.130 --> 00:01:19.520
The stutter chant

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:23.590
Another thing book readers and fans of the
mini-series will recognize is the phrase Bill

00:01:23.590 --> 00:01:25.640
uses to overcome his stutter.

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:30.570
In the book, it's "he thrusts his fists against
the posts and still insists he sees the ghosts."

00:01:30.570 --> 00:01:35.080
In the movie, only a fraction of that is uttered
by Bill, perhaps because he's still struggling

00:01:35.080 --> 00:01:36.150
to finish it.

00:01:36.150 --> 00:01:40.159
Without the second clause, it's a little less
creepy, but the line is still seen as a major

00:01:40.159 --> 00:01:42.000
metaphor for the story as a whole.

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:46.211
And he uses it to regain his self-confidence
and strength in times of trial in the book

00:01:46.211 --> 00:01:48.820
and movie alike, so it's still effective.

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:50.680
I heart Derry

00:01:50.680 --> 00:01:54.320
Patrick Hockstetter's death in the movie is
nothing like it was in the book.

00:01:54.320 --> 00:01:57.369
But there is still something familiar about
his fate.

00:01:57.369 --> 00:02:01.330
In the movie, he comes upon a balloon that
turns around to read "I Heart Derry" on it

00:02:01.330 --> 00:02:02.740
before It gets him.

00:02:02.740 --> 00:02:06.540
It's ominous enough standing alone, but when
you remember the book's story about Adrian

00:02:06.540 --> 00:02:08.229
Mellon, it's even worse.

00:02:08.229 --> 00:02:12.200
In the book, Adrian was a gay man who was
beaten up and flung beneath a bridge by some

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:13.690
homophobic bullies.

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:17.910
While trying to rescue him, his partner saw
the clown and that the overpass was filled

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:20.340
with balloons that had the same phrasing on
it.

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:23.930
And it was a hat with the "I Heart Derry"
logo that started the attack sequence in the

00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:25.290
first place.

00:02:25.290 --> 00:02:28.850
Perhaps the reason the balloon came into play
for Patrick here is that in the books, he

00:02:28.850 --> 00:02:33.230
too exhibited a preference for a same-sex
partner, even though that didn't make it to

00:02:33.230 --> 00:02:34.230
screen.

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:35.470
The corner

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:39.890
Another wink to the source material is contained
in the location of Georgie's travels while

00:02:39.890 --> 00:02:42.150
chasing his paper boat down the street.

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:46.500
The camera pans to the street signs, which
reveal he's at the intersection of Jackson

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:48.370
and Witcham, just like in the book.

00:02:48.370 --> 00:02:52.870
And the fact that he runs right into one of
the sawhorses that littered the flooded street,

00:02:52.870 --> 00:02:56.710
well, that's quite literally a book reference
that smacks us in the face.

00:02:56.710 --> 00:02:58.140
Paul Bunyan

00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:02.099
When the Losers gather to discuss the town's
killer clown in the park, a statue of Paul

00:03:02.099 --> 00:03:06.010
Bunyan can be seen lingering behind Mike as
he fills them in on what he knows.

00:03:06.010 --> 00:03:10.019
In the book, the statue is not only there,
but it also comes to life to taunt Richie

00:03:10.019 --> 00:03:13.420
as his own personalized fear, instead of the
clowns.

00:03:13.420 --> 00:03:16.940
Perhaps the reason the statue still made it
into the movie, despite not being presented

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:21.640
as Richie's materialized fear factor, is that
the statue really does exist in Stephen King's

00:03:21.640 --> 00:03:23.660
neck of the woods in Bangor, Maine.

00:03:23.660 --> 00:03:26.920
Not only that, but it's been creeping all
the locals out​ since it was erected.

00:03:26.920 --> 00:03:30.970
"I know the difference between a bad dream
and real life, okay?"

00:03:30.970 --> 00:03:32.680
The forgetting

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:37.610
After they manage to defeat It for the first
time, the Losers assemble for what is, unbeknownst

00:03:37.610 --> 00:03:40.440
to them, the last time the seven will be together
like this.

00:03:40.440 --> 00:03:43.970
They listen to Beverly as she talks about
what it was like to be under the deadlights

00:03:43.970 --> 00:03:48.760
of Pennywise, and she tells them that she's
already forgetting pieces of what she experienced.

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:51.819
That's a definite bit of foreshadowing for
what's going to come when they grow up and

00:03:51.819 --> 00:03:57.269
purge It and Derry as a whole from their adult
memories… until It returns, of course.

00:03:57.269 --> 00:03:58.269
Exit order

00:03:58.269 --> 00:04:02.680
Another subtle preview exists in the order
in which the Losers take leave of each other,

00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:05.349
with Stan departing first and Eddie shortly
behind him.

00:04:05.349 --> 00:04:09.280
As we know from the book's second half, that's
also the order in which they'll depart in

00:04:09.280 --> 00:04:10.849
entirely different ways.

00:04:10.849 --> 00:04:16.079
Stan, as an adult, is so shaken by the return
of It that he takes his own life in the bathtub.

00:04:16.079 --> 00:04:20.319
And Eddie makes it back to Derry to fight
alongside his childhood comrades but is killed

00:04:20.319 --> 00:04:23.120
in the battle before It is ultimately defeated.

00:04:23.120 --> 00:04:27.059
Those might be stories for ​Chapter Two​,
but ​It​ wasn't afraid to give fans a

00:04:27.059 --> 00:04:28.900
little taste of what's to come here.

00:04:28.900 --> 00:04:30.169
Thanks for watching!

00:04:30.169 --> 00:04:32.830
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:04:32.830 --> 00:04:34.989
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

